# Connect Renovate

Renovate reads the catalogue anonymously using a custom datasource. Private
image locations remain in the consumer repository; the catalogue needs only the
public service alias, tag and digest.

## Configure a consumer

1. Start from the <%= link_to 'Renovate configuration', repository_file('examples/renovate/renovate.json') %>
   and <%= link_to 'synthetic image references', repository_file('examples/renovate/images.yaml') %>.
2. Replace `https://catalogue.example.test` with your catalogue origin and the
   synthetic consumer references with your own image references.
3. Set the dependency name used by the custom datasource to the existing public
   service alias, such as `widget`.
4. Run Renovate against an isolated consumer and review its proposed tag and
   digest update before enabling it on a real repository.

The feed is `GET /api/v1/services/widget/renovate`. Each record supplies the
actual tag as `version`, the complete digest as `digest` and a
`releaseTimestamp`. The supplied regex manager captures both version and digest
so Renovate can propose a new tag plus digest or a changed digest for the same
moving tag.

## Avoid duplicate lookups

The standalone example enables only `custom.regex`. When merging it into an
existing Renovate configuration, preserve the other managers you need and
exclude these exact private packages from Docker managers separately. Otherwise
the same image may also cause a private registry lookup.

## Match your actual tag scheme

The versioned example uses semantic versioning. The `latest` example uses a
literal-tag regex versioning rule, allowing digest-only updates while that tag
stays `latest`. Choose versioning that matches your producer's real tags;
Release Mirror never translates them into a new version scheme.

The repository's integration test exercises the pinned Renovate engine's regex
extraction, HTTP datasource and update selection. A live hosted Renovate
installation remains a separate acceptance step for your deployment.
